drm/vgem: register from the real driver instead of an initcall

DRM hands out primary and render minors from one xarray in registration
order (drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c: drm_minor_get_xa(), drm_minor_alloc(),
DRM_MINOR_LIMIT()), so whoever registers first owns card0 and renderD128.
On this device that ordering decides whether the display works at all.

The ROM's /vendor/etc/selinux/vendor_file_contexts labels exactly three
/dev/dri names as graphics_device - card0, controlD64, renderD128 - and
plat_sepolicy.cil grants graphics_device chr_file to four domains
(surfaceflinger, hal_composer, gpuservice, charger_type) while neverallowing
it to untrusted_app, isolated_app and shell. Anything not named by those
three entries falls back to the `device` type, which no domain may open. So
if vgem registers first it eats card0/renderD128 and the real MSM card lands
on card1/renderD129, where nothing can open it, and the UI goes dark.

Calling vgem_init() from msm's bind path, right after its own
drm_dev_register() succeeded, fixes that without any numbering trick: msm
takes card0, controlD64 and renderD128 (exactly the three names the ROM
already labels), and vgem lands on card1/renderD129. That renderD129 is the
path libgsl.so's gsl_ioctl_init() gates its DRM backend on with
access(..., F_OK), so it only has to exist; ioctl_drm_driver_entry()
readdir()s /dev/dri and picks the card whose DRM_IOCTL_VERSION driver name is
"msm", and it logs and continues when an individual open() fails, which is
what the unreachable vgem node does.

DAC is not in the way: /system/etc/ueventd.rc line 60 is
`/dev/dri/*   0666   root   graphics`, a wildcard, and the stock GKI Image
carries no devtmpfs at all (extract-ikconfig: "# CONFIG_DEVTMPFS is not
set"), so ueventd has always owned these nodes' permissions.

Requires CONFIG_DRM_VGEM=y: the call is in-tree, so a modular vgem would not
link.

---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/vgem/vgem_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/vgem/vgem_drv.c
@@ -124,11 +124,19 @@
 	.minor	= DRIVER_MINOR,
 };
 
-static int __init vgem_init(void)
+/* flame: no initcall any more, and not __init either. The board's real DRM
+ * driver calls this from its bind path once its own card is registered, so
+ * that it keeps card0 and renderD128; a component bind can happen after init
+ * memory is freed, which is why the marking has to go.
+ */
+int vgem_init(void)
 {
 	int ret;
 	struct faux_device *fdev;
 
+	if (vgem_device)
+		return 0;
+
 	fdev = faux_device_create("vgem", NULL, NULL);
 	if (!fdev)
 		return -ENODEV;
@@ -163,7 +171,7 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
-static void __exit vgem_exit(void)
+static void vgem_exit(void)
 {
 	struct faux_device *fdev = vgem_device->faux_dev;
 
@@ -172,7 +180,6 @@
 	faux_device_destroy(fdev);
 }
 
-module_init(vgem_init);
 module_exit(vgem_exit);
 
 MODULE_AUTHOR("Red Hat, Inc.");
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/vgem/vgem_drv.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/vgem/vgem_drv.h
@@ -39,6 +39,14 @@
 	struct mutex fence_mutex;
 };
 
+/*
+ * vgem_init() is normally reached through module_init(). On flame it is called
+ * from msm's component bind instead, so that msm - and not vgem - owns card0
+ * and renderD128, the only DRM nodes the ROM's SELinux labels let any domain
+ * open; see patches/local/0008-drm-vgem-register-from-msm.patch.
+ */
+int vgem_init(void);
+
 int vgem_fence_open(struct vgem_file *file);
 int vgem_fence_attach_ioctl(struct drm_device *dev,
 			    void *data,
